window.AG_CHAPTERS["21"] = {"id":"21","title":"The Voice That Ran Out","titleZh":"聲音用完了","patient":{"name":"Chen the storyteller 說書人陳伯","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-treatment.jpg","key":"day"},"pattern":"Heart and Lung Qi Deficiency 心肺氣虛","principle":"tonify the qi of the Heart and Lung, and secure the exterior","tongue":"pale and slightly swollen, with a thin white coating","pulseNote":{"en":"Present but without strength, and thinnest of all at the near position under your index finger.","zh":"脈來無力，而以寸部最弱"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>T&#224;iyu&#257;n LU9</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"LU9","zh":"太淵","py":"T&#224;iyu&#257;n","x":140,"y":652,"img":"art/body-forearm-palmar.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":60,"y":520,"w":250,"h":300},"decoys":[{"x":201,"y":570},{"x":236,"y":656},{"x":140,"y":609},{"x":112,"y":652},{"x":168,"y":660}],"note":"<b>太淵 T&#224;iyu&#257;n LU9</b> — at the radial end of the wrist crease, in the depression lateral to the radial artery. Source point and Stream point of the Lung, and the Influential point of the Vessels 脈會. You will feel the artery beating beside it; that is the landmark, and it is also the reason the point is what it is.<br>腕橫紋橈側端，橈動脈搏動處之外側凹陷中。肺之原穴、輸穴，八會穴之脈會。指下可覺動脈搏動，此其定位之標，亦其所以為脈會之由。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"He is not ill in an ordinary way. Ask what happens when he <i>works</i>.","zh":"其病不在尋常。當問：勞作之時何如？"},"look":{"en":"Nothing dramatic on this tongue. Absence is the finding.","zh":"此舌無奇。所無者，即其所見。"},"pulse":{"en":"Feel all three positions on both wrists, and notice which one is emptiest.","zh":"兩手三部俱按，察其何部最虛。"},"dx":{"en":"Name the <i>kind</i> of emptiness before you name the organ.","zh":"先定虛之類，後定病之臟。"},"rx":{"en":"Two organs share one room upstairs. Treat the room as well as the tenants.","zh":"二臟同居上焦一室。治其室，亦治其居者。"},"tech":{"en":"There is nothing here to take away.","zh":"此案無可瀉者。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"patient","en":"Chen sits down carefully, and it takes him a moment before he can speak.","zh":"陳伯小心坐下，過了片刻才說得出話。"},{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen does not ask a question yet. He watches him breathe.","zh":"參師父並不即問，先觀其息。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy quietly moves the stool closer so the old man will not have to stand again.","zh":"藥童悄悄把凳子挪近些，免得老人再站起來。"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"Forty years I filled that teahouse without raising my voice. Now I cannot finish a sentence.\"","zh":"「四十年了，我不用扯著嗓子就滿座聽得見。如今一句話都說不完。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Listen to how he ends his sentences, not how he begins them. That is where the qi runs out.\"","zh":"「聽其句末，勿聽其句首。氣盡處在末。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"And look at his shirt. He has been sitting still in a cool room.\"","zh":"「再看其衣。他靜坐於涼室之中。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"channel_organ","look":"excess_def","pulse":"excess_def","dx":"excess_def","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Two Organs, One Room","cardZh":"心肺氣虛 · 上焦同病","cardTxt":"The Heart and the Lung are the only two organs that share the upper burner, and the classics put a single substance between them: gathering qi 宗氣, formed in the chest from the air the Lung takes in and the food-essence the Spleen sends up. Gathering qi does two jobs — it drives respiration, and it pushes the blood through the vessels. So when it is thin, you do not get a Lung complaint or a Heart complaint; you get both at once, in the same patient, made worse by the same thing. Breathlessness and palpitations that arrive together on exertion, a weak voice, spontaneous sweating, and a pulse that is emptiest at the near position where the Heart and Lung are read — that combination is not two diagnoses. It is one.","herbId":"huangqi","herbEn":"Astragalus Root","herbZh":"黃耆","herbImg":"art/card-huangqi.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet and slightly warm, entering Spleen and Lung. It tonifies qi and raises the yang, and — unusually among qi tonics — it reaches the surface, securing the exterior and stopping the daytime sweating that comes of qi failing to hold the pores closed. It is the chief herb of Buzhong Yiqi Tang and of Yu Ping Feng San for exactly these two talents. Not used alone while an exterior pathogen is unresolved, since securing the border before the pathogen has left keeps it inside, and not where heat is from excess."},"endings":{"three":["A month later he sends word that he finished a full evening&#39;s telling and slept well after it. He has cut the programme from three hours to two, on his own.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You heard two complaints and looked for one thing underneath them. That is the whole of it.\"","月餘，遣人來報：說了整整一晚的書，且睡得安穩。他自己把三個時辰的段子減成兩個。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你聽見二病而求其一本。醫道盡於此矣。」"],"two":["The breathlessness is better. The sweating is not.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You tonified. Good. Now go and find the half of the treatment that holds a border rather than fills a store.\"","氣短已減，自汗如故。<br><b>參師父：</b>「補是矣。今且去尋其另半——守邊者，非填倉者也。」"],"one":["He is no better, and he has stopped taking engagements.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You treated the loudest symptom. Emptiness is quiet. That is exactly why it gets missed.\"","不見其效，他已不再接場子。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你治其最響者。虛證無聲——此其所以每為人所遺也。」"],"titles":["Chen Fills the Teahouse Again","Chen Tells a Short Tale","Chen Puts Down the Fan"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"An old man who talks for a living has come in unable to talk. Before you do anything, watch him breathe for a count of ten.","cn":"以言語為業之老人，今不能言而來。未動手前，且觀其呼吸十息。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I tell stories at the Wu family teahouse. Have done since I was nineteen.","cn":"我在吳家茶館說書，十九歲上就開始了。"},{"w":"patient","en":"This winter my voice goes after half an hour. And my heart starts knocking, here, whenever I stand for long.","cn":"今年冬天，說半個時辰嗓子就沒了。站得久了，這裡——心口就砰砰跳。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I sweat through my shirt sitting down. My wife says I am pale as paper.","cn":"我坐著就出一身汗，衣裳都濕。內人說我面色白得像紙。"},{"w":"master","en":"Breathless and palpitating, both worse for effort, in a man with no fever and no pain. Two organs and one cause. Find the cause.","cn":"氣短與心悸並見，皆因勞而甚；其人無熱無痛。二臟一因，當求其因。"}],"qa":[{"q":"When do the palpitations and the breathlessness come on?","cq":"心悸、氣短何時而作？","good":1,"a":"Both together, whenever I exert myself. Climbing the stairs to the upper room does it. Resting settles them.","ca":"二者一同來，一使力就犯。上樓到雅間就犯。歇著便好。","clue":{"en":"Both symptoms worse on exertion, better with rest","zh":"心悸氣短俱因勞而發，得息則減"},"note":"The single most useful question in this case, and it does two things at once. <b>Worse on exertion, better with rest</b> is the signature of a deficiency pattern — an excess pathogen does not care whether you are resting. And the fact that the two symptoms arrive <i>together</i> and leave together says they share a cause rather than being two separate illnesses in one unlucky man. 此案第一要問，一問而得二事。<b>動則甚、靜則減</b>，虛證之定式也——實邪不因勞逸而異。且二證同來同去，知其同本，非一人而兼二病也。"},{"q":"Tell me about the sweating. When, and where?","cq":"其汗何時而出？出於何處？","good":1,"a":"In the daytime, sitting still, no reason for it. Not at night. Chest and back mostly.","ca":"白日靜坐無故而出，夜間則否。多在胸背。","clue":{"en":"Spontaneous daytime sweating at rest","zh":"晝日自汗，靜而汗出"},"note":"Two kinds of sweat and they point opposite ways. <b>Spontaneous sweating</b> 自汗 — in the daytime, at rest, worse with exertion — is qi failing to hold the pores closed, and belongs to qi or yang deficiency. <b>Night sweating</b> 盜汗 — appearing in sleep and stopping on waking — belongs to yin deficiency with heat. Asking <i>when</i> costs one sentence and separates the two. 汗有二端，所指相反。<b>自汗</b>者晝出、靜亦出、動則甚，衛氣不固腠理，屬氣虛陽虛。<b>盜汗</b>者寐中出、醒則止，屬陰虛內熱。但問其時，一語而分。"},{"q":"How is your voice, apart from running out?","cq":"聲音除易竭之外，尚有何異？","good":1,"a":"Low. And I do not want to talk — which for me is a strange thing to say.","ca":"低。而且懶得說話——這話從我嘴裡出來，實在奇怪。","clue":{"en":"Low weak voice, disinclined to speak","zh":"聲低懶言"},"note":"<i>Low voice and disinclination to speak</i> 聲低懶言 is a fixed phrase in the textbooks and a genuinely useful observation: qi deficiency shows in the voice before it shows anywhere a patient would think to mention. Speaking costs qi. A person who is short of it stops spending it. The contrast is a loud, restless, talkative excess-heat patient. 「聲低懶言」為書中定語，亦實可據：氣虛之候，多先見於聲，而病人自不以為病。言者耗氣，氣少者則自省其費。反之，實熱者多聲高多言而躁。"},{"q":"Is there a cough? What comes up?","cq":"咳否？其痰何如？","good":1,"a":"A weak little cough, on and off. Almost nothing comes up, and what does is thin and clear.","ca":"時有輕咳，無力。痰甚少，清稀而已。","clue":{"en":"Weak cough, scant thin clear sputum","zh":"咳而無力，痰少清稀"},"note":"The cough matters less than its <i>character</i>. A weak, ineffective cough with thin clear sputum and no urgency is Lung qi that cannot diffuse and descend properly — not a pathogen being expelled. Compare a productive rattling cough with thick yellow phlegm (damp-heat or phlegm-heat), or a hacking dry cough with a scrap of sticky phlegm (Lung yin depletion). 咳之有無不若咳之為狀。咳而無力、痰少清稀而無急迫，此肺氣不足、宣降失司，非驅邪之咳也。試較之：痰多黏黃而漉漉有聲者，痰熱濕熱；乾咳而痰少黏難出者，肺陰虛。"},{"q":"Do you sleep, and do you dream?","cq":"寐否？多夢否？","good":1,"a":"I fall asleep easily enough. I wake tired, and I am tired all day.","ca":"入睡尚易，醒來卻乏，終日皆乏。","clue":{"en":"Sleeps but wakes unrefreshed; fatigue all day","zh":"能寐而醒後仍疲，終日乏力"},"note":"Useful mostly for what it rules out. Difficulty <i>falling</i> asleep with a racing mind points toward Heart fire or Liver yang; waking at three or four with heat and night sweats points toward yin deficiency; vivid dream-disturbed sleep with palpitations points toward Heart blood deficiency. Sleeping normally and waking unrefreshed is plain qi deficiency, which is the quietest of the four and therefore the easiest to walk past. 此問之用，多在其所排除。難入寐而思慮紛紜者，心火、肝陽；四更而醒兼熱兼盜汗者，陰虛；多夢紛紜而兼悸者，心血虛。今能寐而醒後不復者，單是氣虛——四虛之中最靜者，故亦最易忽。"},{"q":"Have you had fever, chills, or any pain?","cq":"曾發熱惡寒否？有痛否？","good":1,"a":"None of it. I only get smaller.","ca":"都沒有。只是人一天比一天小了。","clue":{"en":"No fever, no chills, no pain","zh":"無寒熱，無疼痛"},"note":"A negative finding, deliberately asked. No fever and no chills rules out an exterior pattern; no pain in a chest complaint is worth having on the record, because chest pain with palpitations would send you looking for blood stasis or phlegm obstruction instead — and that case, unlike this one, can be an emergency. Ask the dangerous question even when you expect the safe answer. 此為有意而問之陰性所見。無寒熱則非表證；胸中之病而無痛，尤當記之——若心悸而兼胸痛，則當求之血瘀、痰阻，而彼證與此不同，或屬危候。雖料其答為安，危問亦不可略。"}],"look":[{"t":"Pale and slightly swollen, thin white coating","c":"舌淡而略胖，苔薄白","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Pale slightly swollen tongue, thin white coat","zh":"舌淡胖，苔薄白"},"note":"Correct — and notice how little there is to see. <b>Pale</b> means qi or blood is not filling the tongue. <b>Slightly swollen</b>, often with faint tooth marks at the edge, means qi is not holding fluid in motion. <b>A thin white coat</b> means no pathogen and no heat: the stomach qi is still making a normal coat, there is simply not much force behind anything. In deficiency the tongue is quiet. Learning to read a quiet tongue is harder than reading a dramatic one, and more often needed. 是——且當察其所見之少。<b>舌淡</b>者，氣血不榮於舌。<b>略胖</b>而邊或有齒痕者，氣不化津。<b>苔薄白</b>者，無邪無熱：胃氣尚能生苔，特其力不足耳。虛證之舌本靜。能讀靜舌，難於讀奇舌，而所用尤多。"},{"t":"Red with little or no coating","c":"舌紅少苔或無苔","ok":0,"note":"Yin deficiency with empty heat. That patient sweats at night, feels hot in the palms and soles in the late afternoon, and is thirsty for small cool sips. This one sweats in the daytime and is pale. 陰虛內熱之舌。彼者盜汗、午後五心煩熱、口乾而喜少飲。此人晝日自汗而面白。"},{"t":"Dark purple, or with stasis spots","c":"舌紫暗，或有瘀點","ok":0,"note":"Blood stasis — and in a chest case that is a reading you must be able to exclude out loud, because stasis in the chest presents with stabbing fixed pain and can be serious. He has no chest pain and his tongue is pale, not purple. 血瘀之舌。胸中之病，此候必當明白排除，蓋瘀阻心脈者痛如針刺、痛有定處，或屬危證。今其人胸不痛，舌淡而不紫。"},{"t":"Pale and swollen with a white slippery coat","c":"舌淡胖，苔白滑","ok":0,"note":"Close, and the difference is the coat. <i>Slippery</i> — visibly wet, water standing on the surface — adds cold or damp to the picture and points toward yang deficiency rather than qi deficiency. Yang deficiency is qi deficiency plus cold: this man is not cold. 近之，其別在苔。<b>滑</b>者望之潤濕，水停舌面，是兼寒兼濕，當屬陽虛而非但氣虛。陽虛者，氣虛而兼寒也；此人不寒。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Weak, and weakest at the cun position 脈弱，寸尤甚","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Weak pulse, emptiest at the cun position","zh":"脈弱，寸部尤弱"},"note":"Correct, and the position is the point. <b>Weak</b> 弱 — present but without strength under any pressure — is the plain pulse of qi deficiency. The <b>cun</b> position, nearest the wrist crease under your index finger, is where the Heart is read on the left and the Lung on the right; both being emptiest there says the upper burner is where the shortage lives. A pulse read in three positions gives you a location as well as a quality, and this is one of the cases where that pays. 是，而所貴在部。<b>弱</b>者舉按皆無力，氣虛之常脈。<b>寸</b>部近腕，食指所按：左寸候心，右寸候肺。二寸俱弱，則知不足在上焦。三部之診，既得其象，兼得其位；此案正見其用。"},{"t":"Thin and rapid 細數","ok":0,"note":"Thin says insufficiency and rapid says heat — together they are the pulse of yin deficiency with empty heat. His pulse is not fast, and he has no heat signs anywhere. 細主不足，數主熱；合則陰虛內熱之脈。此人脈不數，通身無熱候。"},{"t":"Knotted or intermittent 結代","ok":0,"note":"A pulse that pauses is a serious finding in a patient with palpitations and must never be waved away — it points toward Heart qi or Heart yang failing to keep the vessels moving, and it changes the urgency of the case. His pulse is weak but regular. Check for it every time; be glad when it is absent. 脈有歇止，於心悸之人為重候，斷不可略——多屬心氣心陽不能鼓動血脈，其病之緩急由此而異。今其脈弱而不歇。每診必察，無之則幸。"},{"t":"Slippery and forceful 滑而有力","ok":0,"note":"Something substantial has accumulated and the body still has strength to push against it. That is an excess pulse. Nothing in this case is full. 有形之邪積於內，而正氣尚能與之相搏，實脈也。本案無一實候。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — this is a deficiency. But there are four kinds. Which one?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：此屬虛證，然虛有四端。此何虛也？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Qi deficiency — worse on exertion, weak voice, spontaneous daytime sweating, pale tongue, weak pulse","c":"氣虛 — 動則甚、聲低、晝日自汗、舌淡、脈弱","ok":1,"note":"Correct. Run the four against him. <b>Qi deficiency</b>: tired, worse for effort, low voice, spontaneous daytime sweat, pale tongue, weak pulse — he has all six. <b>Blood deficiency</b>: pale <i>and</i> dull complexion, dizziness on standing, dry brittle hair and nails, dream-disturbed sleep, thin pulse — he has almost none of it. <b>Yin deficiency</b>: night sweats, five-palm heat, thirst for small sips, red tongue with no coat, thin rapid pulse — none. <b>Yang deficiency</b>: everything in the qi list <i>plus cold</i> — cold limbs, aversion to cold, clear copious urine, a slow pulse — and he is not cold. Naming the kind before the organ keeps you from treating the right organ in the wrong direction. 是。試以四虛次其人。<b>氣虛</b>：倦、動則甚、聲低、自汗、舌淡、脈弱——六者俱備。<b>血虛</b>：面色淡白無華、起則頭眩、髮爪枯脆、多夢、脈細——幾無一見。<b>陰虛</b>：盜汗、五心煩熱、口乾喜少飲、舌紅無苔、脈細數——無。<b>陽虛</b>：氣虛諸證<b>而兼寒</b>：肢冷、畏寒、小便清長、脈遲——其人不寒。先定虛之類而後定臟，則不致治得其臟而反其向。"},{"t":"Blood deficiency","c":"血虛","ok":0,"note":"Blood deficiency and qi deficiency both produce pallor, and they are easy to confuse — but blood deficiency pallor is dull and lustreless, comes with dizziness on rising, dry hair and nails, and a thin pulse. Sweating is not part of it. Look for what is <i>absent</i> as well as what is present. 血虛、氣虛俱能面白，最易相混。然血虛之白，淡而無華，兼見起則眩、髮爪枯、脈細，而不當有自汗。診法當並察其所無。"},{"t":"Yin deficiency with empty heat","c":"陰虛內熱","ok":0,"note":"That patient sweats at <i>night</i>, has heat in the palms and soles in the late afternoon, a red tongue with no coat and a thin rapid pulse. Every one of those is the opposite of what is in front of you. The sweat alone should have decided it. 彼者盜汗於<b>夜</b>，午後五心煩熱，舌紅無苔，脈細數——一一與此相反。但憑其汗，已足以決之。"},{"t":"Yang deficiency","c":"陽虛","ok":0,"note":"The most defensible wrong answer, because yang deficiency contains qi deficiency. The extra ingredient is cold: cold hands and feet, aversion to cold, clear copious urine, a slow pulse, a wet slippery coat. He has none of that, so stop at qi. Adding warmth he does not need is how a mild tonic becomes a dry one. 此誤最有可說者，蓋陽虛本含氣虛。所增者寒也：手足冷、畏寒、小便清長、脈遲、苔白滑。其人俱無，則止於氣可也。妄增其溫，則和平之補反成燥藥。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — which organs? Two of his complaints belong to two different ones.<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：病在何臟？其二證各屬一臟。</small>","opts":[{"t":"The Heart and the Lung","c":"心與肺","ok":1,"note":"Correct. <b>Palpitations</b> belong to the Heart, which governs the blood and the vessels and houses the spirit. <b>Breathlessness, a weak cough, a low voice and spontaneous sweating</b> belong to the Lung, which governs qi and respiration and controls the exterior through the wei qi. Both organs sit in the upper burner, and both his pulses are emptiest at the cun position, which is where those two are read. The location, the symptoms and the pulse all name the same floor of the house. 是。<b>心悸</b>屬心：心主血脈而藏神。<b>氣短、咳而無力、聲低、自汗</b>屬肺：肺主氣司呼吸，外合皮毛而司衛。二臟同居上焦，而其兩寸俱弱，寸者正候心肺之部。證、位、脈三者同指一層。"},{"t":"The Spleen alone","c":"獨在脾","ok":0,"note":"The Spleen is genuinely involved — it makes the qi that the chest gathers, and treating it is part of the answer. But the Spleen&#39;s own picture is poor appetite, abdominal distension after eating, loose stools and heavy limbs, and he reports none of those. Do not promote a contributing organ to the diagnosis. 脾誠有涉——氣之所生在脾，治之亦其一端。然脾病自有其候：納呆、食後腹脹、便溏、四肢困重，其人皆無。佐助之臟，不可升為病位。"},{"t":"The Kidney","c":"腎","ok":0,"note":"Kidney qi failing to grasp qi does cause breathlessness — but that breathlessness is worse on <i>inhalation</i>, comes with a weak sore low back and knees, and often with urinary changes. His shortness of breath is on exertion, and his back is fine. Save the Kidney for the case that shows it. 腎不納氣誠能致喘——然其喘以<b>吸</b>為難，兼腰膝痠軟，多兼小便之變。此人氣短因勞而作，腰膝無恙。腎虛之診，當俟其證見而後言。"},{"t":"The Liver","c":"肝","ok":0,"note":"Nothing here is stagnant, hot, or emotionally constrained: no rib-side fullness, no sighing, no irritability, no wiry pulse. The Liver is the organ students reach for when a case is unclear; this case is clear. 本案無鬱、無熱、無情志之結：脅無脹滿、無太息、無煩躁、脈不弦。案不明時，學者每歸之於肝；此案明矣。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — two organs, one shortage. What connects them, and what does that mean for treatment?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：二臟一虛，其所以相連者何？於治法有何謂？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Gathering qi 宗氣 collects in the chest and does both jobs — it drives respiration and pushes blood through the vessels; treat the shortage once, upstream of both","c":"宗氣聚於胸中，上走息道而行呼吸，貫心脈而行血氣；當治其一本，不分治其二證","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and this is the lesson of the chapter. Gathering qi 宗氣 is formed in the chest from the clear qi the Lung draws in and the food-essence the Spleen sends up. The classics give it two functions and they are exactly this man&#39;s two complaints: it 走息道以行呼吸 — travels the airways to drive breathing — and it 貫心脈以行氣血 — penetrates the heart vessels to move qi and blood. One substance, two organs, two symptoms, one treatment. Practically this means: tonify Lung qi, support the Heart, and strengthen the Spleen that supplies both, rather than writing one prescription for the cough and another for the palpitations. 是，本章之旨在此。宗氣者，積於胸中，肺所吸之清氣與脾所升之穀氣合而成之。經言其功有二，適即其人之二證：<b>走息道以行呼吸</b>、<b>貫心脈以行氣血</b>。一氣、二臟、二證，而治其一。故其法為：補肺氣、益心氣、健其脾以資二者之源，而不當一方治咳、一方治悸。"},{"t":"They are two separate illnesses that happen to have arrived together","c":"二病偶合，各自為患","ok":0,"note":"Possible in principle, and here the evidence is against it: the two symptoms arrive together, worsen together with the same trigger and settle together with rest. Things that move as one usually are one. 理固有之，而據不支：二證同來、同因勞而甚、同得息而減。同進同退者，多是一物。"},{"t":"The Lung is the mother of the Heart in the five-phase cycle, so treat the mother","c":"肺為心之母，當補其母","ok":0,"note":"Check the cycle before leaning on it. In the generating sequence it runs Wood → Fire → Earth → Metal → Water, so Fire (Heart) generates Earth, and Metal (Lung) is generated <i>by</i> Earth and generates Water. The Lung is not the mother of the Heart — if anything, Fire restrains Metal. The five phases are a good tool and a bad habit; verify the relationship before you invoke it. 引五行者，當先驗其序。相生之序：木→火→土→金→水。火（心）生土，金（肺）為土所生而生水。肺非心之母也；若論相剋，則火剋金。五行為良具，亦易成惰習。援之當先審之。"},{"t":"Both are downstream of Kidney essence, so tonify the Kidney and the rest follows","c":"二者皆本於腎精，補腎則餘自復","ok":0,"note":"A true statement made at the wrong altitude. Everything is downstream of the Kidney eventually, which is precisely why that reasoning cannot distinguish one case from another. Treat the level where the evidence is. 語雖不誤，而所論之層太高。萬病終可歸之於腎，正以其能歸萬病，故不足以別此案與彼案。當治其有據之層。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"LU9","t":"T&#224;iyu&#257;n LU9 太淵","ok":1,"note":"The Source point of the Lung, which is where you go to supplement a depleted organ through its own channel — and, uniquely, the Influential point of the Vessels 脈會. That second role is why it is the right needle for <i>this</i> case rather than merely a correct one: the Lung 朝百脈 receives all the vessels, the Heart 主血脈 governs them, and Taiyuan is the one point that sits at the meeting of the two ideas. You can feel the radial artery beating beside it while you locate it. 肺之原穴，補臟虛者取其原；而又為八會之脈會。後者所以使此穴於<b>本案</b>不僅為是，而為最當：肺朝百脈，心主血脈，而太淵正當二義之交。定穴之際，指旁可覺橈動脈之搏。"},{"id":"CV17","t":"D&#224;nzh&#333;ng CV17 膻中","ok":1,"note":"The Influential point of qi 氣會, on the midline of the chest level with the fourth intercostal space, and the Front-Mu point of the Pericardium. It sits precisely where gathering qi collects, which makes it the anatomical statement of this whole diagnosis. Needled transversely and shallowly, never perpendicular and never deep — the sternum is under it and the lung is beside it. 八會之氣會，在胸前正中線，平第四肋間，兼心包之募穴。其位正當宗氣所聚，故此穴即本診之形證。當平刺淺刺，切忌直刺深刺——其下為胸骨，其旁為肺。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":1,"note":"Reinforcing earth to generate metal 培土生金. Gathering qi is made from what the Spleen sends up, so a chest that keeps running out of qi has a supply problem as well as a storage problem. This is the point that treats the supply, and moxa on it is well suited to a deficiency case with no heat. 培土生金。宗氣賴脾之所升而成，故胸中屢竭者，既病其藏，亦病其供。此穴治其供也。虛而無熱，尤宜加灸。"},{"id":"LU5","t":"Ch&#464;z&#233; LU5 尺澤","ok":0,"note":"The Sea point of the Lung and a fine point — for draining Lung heat, for a full cough with thick phlegm, for rebellious Lung qi. It clears and descends. This man needs filling, not clearing, and the Sea point is the wrong end of the channel for that job. 肺之合穴，良穴也——然所主為瀉肺熱、痰壅之實咳、肺氣上逆，其用在清在降。此人當補不當清，且補虛取原，不取合。"},{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":0,"note":"Courses the Liver and subdues ascending yang. There is no constraint here, no irritability, no rib-side fullness and no wiry pulse — and moving qi in a patient who is short of it spends what little he has. 疏肝、平肝潛陽。本案無鬱、無煩躁、無脅脹、脈不弦。且氣本不足而行之，是耗其所餘。"},{"id":"SP9","t":"Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n SP9 陰陵泉","ok":0,"note":"The great damp-draining point. There is no damp: the coat is thin and white, not greasy; the stool is normal; the limbs are weak rather than heavy. Draining a patient who is empty is the commonest way to make a mild case worse. 利濕之要穴。本案無濕：苔薄白而不膩，二便如常，肢乏而不重。虛而利之，輕證轉重，多由於此。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Reinforcing method, with moxa","c":"補法，可灸","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct.</b> Reinforcing, because every finding in the case says empty: worse on exertion, weak voice, spontaneous sweating, pale tongue, weak pulse, no pain, no fever, nothing that refuses the hand. And moxa is welcome here — there is no heat anywhere to forbid it, and gentle warmth supports qi without adding the heat that would make a yin-deficient patient worse. Use few needles, retain them gently, and stop before the patient is tired; a depleted person can be exhausted by vigorous treatment itself. 是。補者，通案皆虛：動則甚、聲低、自汗、舌淡、脈弱、無痛、無熱、無拒按者。灸亦相宜：通身無熱以禁之，而溫和之火能益其氣，不若治陰虛者之慮其助熱。當少針、輕留、未疲而止——大虛之人，每為過治所困。"},{"t":"Reducing method, to open the chest","c":"瀉法，以開其胸","ok":0,"note":"Opening implies something is blocking, and nothing is. A tight chest from qi stagnation or phlegm feels full and resists pressure; his chest is empty and welcomes a hand. Reducing an empty patient removes the little he has. 言開者，必有所塞；本案無塞。氣滯痰阻之胸，滿而拒按；此人胸空而喜按。虛而瀉之，是奪其僅存。"},{"t":"Even method, and see him again in a month","c":"平補平瀉，一月後再診","ok":0,"note":"Even method is for mixed or unclear patterns, and this one is neither. The month is the worse half of the answer: a deficiency case needs frequent short treatments to build, and a month of silence in a seventy-year-old with palpitations is a long time to learn nothing. 平法用於虛實夾雜或未明之證；此案二者皆非。其失尤在「一月」：虛證宜頻診而輕治以漸積其功；且七旬之人而見心悸，一月不聞消息，所失非小。"},{"t":"Strong stimulation to wake the qi up","c":"重刺激以振其氣","ok":0,"note":"A reasonable-sounding instinct and a real trap. Strong stimulation disperses; in a full pattern that is the point, and in an empty one it scatters what remains. The patient will often feel briefly better and then markedly worse the next day. Gentle and repeated beats strong and occasional in every deficiency case. 其說似有理而實為陷。重刺激者主散：實則當之，虛則散其所存。病人每覺一時之爽，翌日反劇。凡虛證，輕而屢者恆勝於重而疏者。"}]};
